<figure class="commit-figure" aria-labelledby="commit-title">
 <figcaption id="commit-title"><span>Figure 3</span> A recoverable purchase before the page files catch up</figcaption>
 <div class="commit-scene">
  <div class="commit-row"><b>1 · Change in memory</b><div><span class="location">Database memory · lost on a crash</span><p>P7: stock 1 → 0<br/>Order 103 + its lines</p></div></div>
  <div class="commit-row"><b>2 · Flush the log</b><div class="durable"><span class="location">Persistent storage · survives this crash</span><p>Records describing the changes<br/>+ the transaction’s commit record</p></div></div>
  <p class="boundary">The log is durable before success is sent ↓</p>
  <div class="commit-row"><b>3 · Reply to application</b><div><p><code>COMMIT</code> succeeded<br/><strong>A lost reply leaves the application uncertain.</strong></p></div></div>
  <div class="commit-row"><b>4 · Write page files later</b><div><p>Changed table and index pages reach their files.<br/>A crash before this step uses the log to recover.</p></div></div>
 </div>
 <p class="figure-note">A conceptual PostgreSQL commit with normal durable settings and storage that honours flushes. The log may be written earlier; this drawing shows the ordering that matters. It leaves out other transactions and replication. These are steps, not equal periods of time.</p>
</figure>
